alex: Quick sync — PR https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/462 landed some 429 guidance but reviewers flagged the SDK example as confusing for timeouts + context-trim fallbacks. Can someone help rewrite a concise example we can ship?

maria: I skimmed it. The existing snippet retries forever without trimming context or switching to a cheaper model. We should show a pragmatic pattern: 1) try a short retry with exponential backoff, 2) if still 429 -> trim context + retry, 3) if still failing -> fallback to a smaller model or cached response. Keep it ~8 lines. :thumbsup:

jonathan: +1. Also call out status codes vs rate-limit headers (X-RateLimit-Reset) and when to respect Retry-After. People confuse 429 and 504 handling.

alex: Good note. Let's include a short Python snippet and a tiny JS/TS variant. Also add a one-liner about honoring Retry-After header. I'll push a suggested edit.

alex: Proposed Python (drop into Troubleshooting/429s):
```python
# simple retry + context trim example
import time
from redwood import RedwoodClient
client = RedwoodClient(api_key=...)

def call_with_fallback(prompt, model_primary, model_fallback):
    for attempt in range(3):
        resp = client.generate(model=model_primary, prompt=prompt, timeout=5)
        if resp.status == 200:
            return resp.text
        if resp.status == 429:
            wait = 2 ** attempt
            time.sleep(wait)
    # last-ditch: trim context and try cheaper model once
    short_prompt = prompt.split('\n')[-3:]  # keep last 3 turns
    return client.generate(model=model_fallback, prompt='\n'.join(short_prompt), timeout=5).text
```

maria: That's clear. For docs we should annotate that trimming should be deterministic and tested with eval prompts (avoid accidentally dropping critical instructions). Add a note: "prefer prompt templates that separate system/instruction from chat history so trimming is safe."

jonathan: For JS snippet, show Fetch + Retry-After handling. Something like:
```js
// pseudocode for fetch + honor Retry-After
async function callApi(payload){
  for(let i=0;i<2;i++){
    const res = await fetch('/v1/generate', {method:'POST', body:JSON.stringify(payload)});
    if(res.status === 200) return await res.json();
    if(res.status === 429){
      const ra = res.headers.get('Retry-After');
      const wait = ra ? Number(ra) : Math.pow(2,i)*1000;
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, wait));
    }
  }
  // fallback to cached answer or cheaper endpoint
  return fetch('/v1/generate?model=small-1', {method:'POST', body:JSON.stringify(trimPayload(payload))});
}
```

sam: tiny nit — use ms in waits consistently and mention aborts for timeouts (AbortController) in the JS example.

alex: Will add an inline comment about AbortController for timeouts and include a link to our SDK's retry helper: https://docs.redwood.ai/sdk/retries (we should make sure that link exists).

sophia (docs-bot): Build status for PR #462: checks/ci-docs — failed (link: https://ci.redwood.ai/462) — failing lint in code sample formatting.

maria: I can fix formatting and add the trimming guideline. Will push a tiny PR against 462 with the updated snippets.

alex: Awesome — please also add a one-line migration note under "Troubleshooting" that older clients that only used naive retries may see increased 429s; recommend upgrading to the trim-then-fallback pattern.

maria: On it. I'll attach a short test case in the PR to validate the trimming logic with the eval harness. :eyes:

jonathan: Once it's in, can eng-runtime review to confirm the fallback to smaller models is compatible with routing/fallback policies? Ping me and I can loop them in.

alex: Done. Thanks all. I'll watch the PR.
